## Description

The authorization check in `hasPermission` only ever returns `false` when a path **matches** a rule whose role list excludes the caller. Any path that matches **no** rule falls through to the trailing `return true`, so RBAC is **fail-open**: an unmapped endpoint authorizes every authenticated role.

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:58-66
function hasPermission(role: UserRole, path: string): boolean {
  for (const [pattern, allowedRoles] of Object.entries(ROUTE_PERMISSIONS)) {
    const regex = new RegExp("^" + pattern.replace(/\*/g, ".*") + "$");
    if (regex.test(path) && !allowedRoles.includes(role)) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true; // no matching rule -> ALLOWED
}
```

The pattern `/api/agents/*` is compiled to `^/api/agents/.*$`, which requires a trailing slash followed by at least zero characters **after** `/api/agents/`. It therefore does **not** match the bare collection route `/api/agents` — which is exactly the registered list endpoint (`app.get("/")` mounted at `/api/agents` in `packages/api/src/routes/agents.ts:28`). Because no rule matches `/api/agents`, the fail-open default applies and a `readonly` or `plugin` token can list agents even though `ROUTE_PERMISSIONS` intends to restrict `/api/agents/*` to `admin`/`user`.

Finally, patterns are interpolated straight into a `RegExp` after replacing only `*`; no other regex metacharacters are escaped. The current patterns contain only `/` and `*`, but any future pattern containing `.`, `+`, `(`, etc. would silently behave as a regex rather than a literal path segment.

## Impact

Authorization can be bypassed two ways: (1) every endpoint not explicitly listed in `ROUTE_PERMISSIONS` is reachable by any authenticated role (fail-open), and (2) the bare `/api/agents` collection endpoint is reachable by `readonly`/`plugin` tokens that should be denied. As new routes are added, the fail-open default makes it easy to ship an endpoint with no access control at all. This is a broken-access-control defect on the primary API surface, warranting **high** severity.

## Required Fix

1. Make the check **fail-closed**: require an explicit matching rule. If no pattern matches the path under `/api/*`, deny by default (return `false`) instead of returning `true`.
2. Match both the collection and its sub-paths, e.g. compile `/api/agents/*` to `^/api/agents(/.*)?$` (or register an explicit `/api/agents` rule) so the bare path is covered.
3. Escape regex metacharacters in the literal portion of each pattern before substituting the `*` wildcard (e.g. escape `[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]` first, then replace the escaped `*`).
4. Add tests asserting that an unmapped path and the bare `/api/agents` path are both denied for unauthorized roles.

## References

- OWASP Top 10 2021 — A01:2021 Broken Access Control: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/
- CWE-285: Improper Authorization: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html
- CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html

## Description

The stricter authentication rate limiter keys each bucket on the raw `x-forwarded-for` request header with no trusted-proxy gate:

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/security.middleware.ts:116-127
return async (ctx: Context, next: Next) => {
  const ip = ctx.req.header("x-forwarded-for") ?? "unknown";
  const path = ctx.req.path;

  let status;
  if (path === "/api/auth/login") {
    status = loginLimiter.consume(`auth:login:${ip}`);
  } else if (path === "/api/auth/refresh") {
    status = refreshLimiter.consume(`auth:refresh:${ip}`);
  } else {
    return next();
  }
```

`X-Forwarded-For` is fully attacker-controlled. By sending a different header value on each request (`X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.<n>`), a client gets a fresh rate-limit bucket every time, so the per-IP login/refresh cap (default 5 / 15 min) never trips. Notably, the general rate-limit middleware in the same file already guards against this via `getClientIP` (lines 34-53), which only honors `X-Forwarded-For` when `TRUSTED_PROXIES` is set — but this auth-specific limiter ignores that helper entirely.

## Impact

The control whose entire purpose is to stop credential brute-force and stuffing on `/api/auth/login` and `/api/auth/refresh` is trivially defeated by header rotation. Combined with the dev login that mints tokens for any credentials, this removes the only throttle on the authentication surface. **High** severity for an auth-hardening control that is bypassable with a single header.

## Required Fix

1. Derive the key from `getClientIP(ctx)` (the existing trusted-proxy-aware helper) instead of reading `x-forwarded-for` directly.
2. Only trust `X-Forwarded-For` when the immediate peer is a configured trusted proxy (`TRUSTED_PROXIES`); otherwise key on the real socket address / `x-real-ip`.
3. Add a test that rotating `X-Forwarded-For` does not reset the auth limiter when no trusted proxy is configured.

## References

- OWASP Top 10 2021 — A07:2021 Identification and Authentication Failures: https://owasp.org/Top10/A07_2021-Identification_and_Authentication_Failures/
- CWE-307: Improper Restriction of Excessive Authentication Attempts: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/307.html
- CWE-290: Authentication Bypass by Spoofing: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/290.html

## Description

`createCorsConfig` exists and is exported from the package index (`packages/api/src/index.ts:10`), but `createServer` never calls it and never registers Hono's `cors()` middleware. The assembled middleware stack contains no CORS handling at all:

```ts
// packages/api/src/server.ts:131-140
// Global middleware
app.use("*", requestIdMiddleware());
app.use("*", securityHeadersMiddleware());
app.use("*", createBodySizeLimitMiddleware(config.security));
app.use("*", createRateLimitMiddleware(config.security));
app.use("/api/auth/*", createAuthRateLimitMiddleware(config.authRateLimit));
app.use("/api/*", createAuthMiddleware(config.auth));
app.use("/api/*", createCsrfMiddleware(config.csrf));
```

`config.security.corsOrigins` is plumbed all the way through (`SecurityConfig.corsOrigins`, populated from `config.api.cors` in `apps/agent/src/index.ts`), and `createCorsConfig` builds a complete Hono CORS options object — but its return value is never consumed (`grep` shows the only references are its definition and the re-export). `hono/cors` is imported only in the V1 webui, never in the V2 API.

## Impact

The configured CORS policy (allowed origins, methods, headers, `exposeHeaders`, `maxAge`) is not enforced. No `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` / preflight handling is emitted by the API, so the operator's allow-list is silently ignored: browsers fall back to the default same-origin policy and the documented cross-origin frontend (`http://localhost:5173`) cannot call the API, while the explicit origin restriction the config implies is never actually applied. This is a config-versus-behavior mismatch on a security-relevant control. **Medium** severity.

## Required Fix

1. In `createServer`, build the options with `createCorsConfig(config.security.corsOrigins)` and register `app.use("*", cors(corsOptions))` (from `hono/cors`) ahead of the auth middleware.
2. Ensure preflight (`OPTIONS`) requests are handled before auth/CSRF short-circuit them.
3. Add a test asserting `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` reflects an allowed origin and is absent for a disallowed one.

## References

- OWASP — CORS misconfiguration / origin handling guidance: https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/CORS_OriginHeaderScrutiny
- MDN — Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS): https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS

## Description

The request body-size guard rejects a request only when a numeric `Content-Length` header is present and exceeds the limit:

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/security.middleware.ts:195-207
export function createBodySizeLimitMiddleware(config: SecurityConfig) {
  const limit = config.maxBodySize ?? 1_048_576; // default 1 MB
  return async (ctx: Context, next: Next): Promise<Response | void> => {
    const contentLength = ctx.req.header("content-length");
    if (contentLength !== undefined && parseInt(contentLength, 10) > limit) {
      return ctx.json(
        { error: { code: "PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE", message: "Request body exceeds size limit" } },
        413 as never
      );
    }
    await next();
  };
}
```

The check is skipped whenever `Content-Length` is absent — which is exactly the case for `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` requests, where a client can stream an unbounded body. A malformed/non-numeric header is equally unguarded: `parseInt("abc", 10)` is `NaN`, and `NaN > limit` is `false`, so the request passes. The actual bytes read downstream are never measured against the limit.

## Impact

The DoS guard the middleware advertises ("Guards against DoS attacks via large payloads") is bypassable: an attacker uses chunked transfer encoding (or simply omits/garbles `Content-Length`) to send arbitrarily large bodies, risking memory exhaustion when handlers call `ctx.req.json()`/`text()`. **Medium** severity (availability).

## Required Fix

1. Enforce the cap on the actual bytes read, not the advertised header — e.g. read the body through a counting stream and abort once `limit` bytes are exceeded, or use the runtime's enforced max-body option.
2. Treat a missing or non-numeric `Content-Length` on requests that carry a body as untrusted and still apply the streamed byte cap.
3. Add tests for a chunked over-limit request and for a non-numeric `Content-Length`.

## References

- OWASP — Denial of Service / unrestricted resource consumption: https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Denial_of_Service
- CWE-770: Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html

## Description

Each `startServer` path attaches only a success callback to `.listen()` and wraps it in a Promise that exposes only `resolve`:

```ts
// packages/api/src/server.ts:118-123
await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
  createAdaptorServer({ fetch: app.fetch }).listen(config.port, config.host, () => {
    console.log(`✅ HTTP server listening on http://${config.host}:${config.port}`);
    resolve();
  });
});
```

The same shape is used for the HTTPS server (lines 85-94) and the HTTP→HTTPS redirect server (lines 99-112). Node's `server.listen()` reports failures such as `EADDRINUSE` or `EACCES` asynchronously via an `'error'` event on the server, not by throwing. With no `.on("error", reject)` handler, that event has no listener: the listen callback never fires, the wrapping Promise never resolves or rejects (`startServer` hangs forever), and the unhandled `'error'` event is thrown by the EventEmitter, crashing the process.

## Impact

A common, recoverable startup condition (port already in use, insufficient privileges for a low port) turns into either a hung `await startServer(...)` or a hard process crash with an unhandled `'error'` — instead of a clean, catchable rejection the caller could log and act on. There is also no `reject` path at all, so callers cannot distinguish "started" from "failed to start". **Medium** severity (reliability / startup robustness).

## Required Fix

1. Capture the server instance and attach `.on("error", reject)` before/alongside the listen callback in all three Promises, and `resolve` only from the listen callback.
2. Surface the rejection from `startServer` so the caller can handle it (and ensure the agent bootstrap awaits and reports it).
3. Add a test that starting two servers on the same port rejects rather than hangs or crashes.

## References

- Node.js `net.Server` `'error'` event (EADDRINUSE handling): https://nodejs.org/api/net.html#event-error

## Description

`ValidationError` carries a structured `details: Record<string, string[]>` of per-field messages (`packages/core/src/errors/domain-errors.ts:24-31`), and the `ErrorResponse` type already declares an optional `details` field. But the handler builds the response body from only `code` and `message`, dropping `details`:

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/error-handler.ts:18-31
export function errorHandler(err: Error, ctx: Context): Response {
  const requestId = ctx.get("requestId") as string | undefined;

  if (err instanceof DomainError) {
    const body: ErrorResponse = {
      error: {
        code: err.code,
        message: err.message,
      },
      requestId,
    };
    return ctx.json(body, err.statusCode as 400 | 401 | 403 | 404 | 429 | 500);
  }
```

Because the handler narrows on `DomainError` (the base class) and never checks for `ValidationError`, the `details` map is never read or serialized.

## Impact

Clients receive a generic 400 with no indication of which field failed or why, making the API hard to integrate against and pushing developers toward trial-and-error. The information needed for actionable validation errors is computed and then thrown away. **Medium** severity (developer experience / error-handling correctness).

## Required Fix

1. In `errorHandler`, special-case `ValidationError` (`err instanceof ValidationError`) and copy `err.details` into `body.error.details`.
2. Keep `details` limited to validation metadata (field → messages); continue to omit internals for unknown errors.
3. Add a test asserting a `ValidationError` response includes the per-field `details`.

## References

- RFC 9457 — Problem Details for HTTP APIs (structured error fields): https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457

## Description

The login handler performs no credential verification. It validates only the *shape* of the body, then issues a signed token for whatever `username` was supplied — granting `admin` when the username happens to be `"admin"`:

```ts
// packages/api/src/routes/auth.ts:85-96
const { username, password: _password } = result.data;

// TODO: Replace with real user store lookup and password hashing
const role = username === "admin" ? "admin" : "user";

const token = await createToken(username, role, config.jwtSecret, config.tokenExpiry);
const refreshToken = await createToken(
  username, role, config.jwtSecret, config.refreshTokenExpiry,
);
```

The password is destructured into `_password` and never used: there is no user store, no password hash comparison, nothing. Any request with a non-empty `username`/`password` of valid length receives a working token, and `username: "admin"` yields a full admin token.

## Impact

Anyone who can reach the API can authenticate as any user — including `admin` — by sending arbitrary credentials, defeating authentication entirely. The handler's own comment marks it as alpha/dev behavior, but there is no environment guard preventing it from running in production. **Medium** severity here on the assumption it is a known pre-production placeholder; it becomes **critical** the moment the service is exposed, so it must be gated before any non-dev deployment.

## Required Fix

1. Replace the stub with a real user-store lookup and constant-time password-hash verification (e.g. argon2/bcrypt); return 401 on mismatch.
2. Until a real store exists, hard-fail (refuse to start, or return 501) when `NODE_ENV === "production"`, so the bypass cannot ship.
3. Never derive role from the raw username; assign roles from the authenticated user record.
4. Add tests for invalid credentials (401) and for production-mode refusal.

## References

- OWASP Top 10 2021 — A07:2021 Identification and Authentication Failures: https://owasp.org/Top10/A07_2021-Identification_and_Authentication_Failures/
- CWE-287: Improper Authentication: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html
- CWE-489: Active Debug Code: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/489.html

## Description

The agent bootstrap builds `serverConfig` with no `csrf` field at all, even though it knows whether TLS is enabled (`config.api.tls`):

```ts
// apps/agent/src/index.ts:62-76
const serverConfig = {
  port: config.api.port,
  host: config.api.host,
  auth: { jwtSecret: config.security.jwtSecret, tokenExpiry: 3600, refreshTokenExpiry: 604800 },
  security: {
    rateLimitWindow: config.security.rateLimitWindow,
    rateLimitMax: config.security.rateLimitMax,
    corsOrigins: config.api.cors ?? [],
  },
  tls: config.api.tls,        // TLS known here, but never used to derive secureCookie
};
```

With `config.csrf` undefined, `createCsrfMiddleware`/`setCsrfCookie` fall back to `secureCookie = false` (`packages/api/src/middleware/csrf.middleware.ts:68,119`), so the `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie is emitted without the `Secure` attribute even when the server is running under HTTPS:

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/csrf.middleware.ts:119-125
const { secureCookie = false, sameSite = "Strict" } = config;
const secure = secureCookie ? "; Secure" : "";
ctx.header("Set-Cookie",
  `${CSRF_COOKIE_NAME}=${token}; Path=/; SameSite=${sameSite}; Max-Age=${maxAge}${secure}`);
```

## Impact

The CSRF cookie can be sent over plaintext HTTP and is exposed to network-level interception/leakage on any non-HTTPS hop, even when the deployment uses TLS. The `Secure` flag is the standard defense and is never set because it is not derived from the TLS/environment configuration. **Medium** severity (cookie-security hardening on a security token).

## Required Fix

1. Derive `secureCookie` from the deployment: set it to `true` whenever TLS is configured (`config.api.tls`) or `NODE_ENV === "production"`, and pass a `csrf` block into `serverConfig`.
2. Plumb `config.csrf` through `createServer` to both `createCsrfMiddleware` and the `/api/auth/csrf-token` cookie issuer so the flag is consistent.
3. Add a test asserting the `Set-Cookie` for `XSRF-TOKEN` includes `Secure` under TLS/production config.

## References

- OWASP — SameSite/Secure cookie attributes (CSRF Cheat Sheet): https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html
- CWE-614: Sensitive Cookie in HTTPS Session Without 'Secure' Attribute: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/614.html

## Description

The V2 agent bootstrap installs only `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` handlers, and the shutdown routine flips a flag and exits without closing the HTTP server, the SQLite repositories, or the event bus:

```ts
// apps/agent/src/index.ts:85-94
// Handle graceful shutdown
const shutdown = async () => {
  console.log("\n🛑 Shutting down...");
  this.running = false;
  process.exit(0);
};

process.on("SIGINT", shutdown);
process.on("SIGTERM", shutdown);
```

There are no `process.on("unhandledRejection", ...)` or `process.on("uncaughtException", ...)` handlers anywhere in the V2 tree (the V1 entrypoints register them, e.g. `v1-src/index.ts:1407` and `v1-src/api/bootstrap.ts:131`, but V2 does not). The created `AgentRuntime`/`AgentOrchestrator`, the server returned by `startServer`, and the `better-sqlite3`-backed repositories are never closed on shutdown.

## Impact

An unhandled promise rejection or uncaught exception terminates the process abruptly with no logging or cleanup, and even an orderly `SIGTERM` skips graceful teardown — in-flight requests are dropped, the listening socket and SQLite connections are not closed (risking a locked/uncheckpointed DB file), and event-bus subscribers are not detached. This undermines reliable operation under container orchestrators that send `SIGTERM` before `SIGKILL`. **Medium** severity (reliability / resource cleanup).

## Required Fix

1. Register top-level `process.on("unhandledRejection", ...)` and `process.on("uncaughtException", ...)` handlers that log and exit non-zero.
2. Have `shutdown` await closing the HTTP server, the SQLite repositories, and the event bus (return a handle from `startServer` and `close()` the underlying server) before `process.exit`.
3. Add a timeout so shutdown still exits if cleanup hangs.

## References

- Node.js process events — `unhandledRejection` / `uncaughtException`: https://nodejs.org/api/process.html#event-unhandledrejection
- Node.js `net.Server.close()` (graceful shutdown): https://nodejs.org/api/net.html#serverclosecallback

## Description

The request-id middleware trusts a client-supplied `x-request-id` verbatim, storing it in the context and echoing it back in a response header:

```ts
// packages/api/src/middleware/security.middleware.ts:212-219
export function requestIdMiddleware() {
  return async (ctx: Context, next: Next) => {
    const requestId = ctx.req.header("x-request-id") ?? crypto.randomUUID();
    ctx.set("requestId", requestId);
    ctx.header("X-Request-Id", requestId);
    await next();
  };
}
```

The value is neither length-bounded nor character-validated. It then flows into the error handler's JSON body and (per the middleware's "for tracing" purpose) into logs (`error-handler.ts:19,26`), and is reflected into the `X-Request-Id` response header.

## Impact

An attacker controls a value that lands in logs and response headers. Newlines/control characters enable log forging/injection (splitting or spoofing log lines in downstream collectors), and an over-long or crafted value bloats logs and is reflected unmodified to the client. **Low** severity (log shaping / header reflection; header-splitting itself is mitigated by Hono's header API, but log injection and unbounded reflection remain).

## Required Fix

1. Validate a supplied `x-request-id` against a strict allowlist (e.g. `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,128}$`); otherwise generate a fresh `crypto.randomUUID()`.
2. Ensure structured logging escapes/encodes the field rather than concatenating it into log lines.
3. Add a test that a malicious `x-request-id` (newlines / over-length) is replaced with a generated id.

## References

- OWASP — Log Injection: https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Log_Injection
- CWE-117: Improper Output Neutralization for Logs: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/117.html
- CWE-20: Improper Input Validation: https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html
